 | Wave size | **Most of the game's shaders are wave64: 23,841 of 26,503** (`fssm_header.wave_size()`: FSSM header flags word `u32[0xD4]` bit 5, `0x20` = wave32, `0` = wave64; 0 contradictions against GFX10 code-proven wave32/wave64 instructions across all 26,503 blobs): 10,974 of 12,101 PS, 9,282 of 9,282 mesh shaders, 2,236 of 2,236 VS, 1,154 of 2,689 CS and 195 of 195 GS, against 2,662 wave32 (1,535 CS and 1,127 PS). A full-corpus translation at `ec8ec4c9` marks exactly these 23,841 `wave64` (`subgroup_size` 64) in the index. Before the FSSM flag (#1900) the wave size came from code markers (64-bit EXEC/mask writes, `exec_hi`, `*_saveexec_b64`): at `6a6704df` they made 21,538 of the 26,487 shaders translated then wave64 (the figure quoted here was 21,545). 10,031 PS, 9,282 mesh shaders, 1,179 VS, 851 CS and 195 GS (the 16 shaders refused then are all wave64 CS by the flag; the figure quoted here was 21,545). Against the flag they call 2,287 wave64 shaders wave32 (943 PS, 1,057 VS, 287 CS), whose code has no 64-bit EXEC/mask marker, and none the other way. An older marker scan (`s_mov_b64 exec, -1`, `exec_hi`, `s_and/or_saveexec_b64` and 64-bit masks) counted 9,930 PS, 8,666 mesh shaders, 1,115 VS and 923 CS, but it left out the 769 listings whose names start with `.` (616 of them mesh shaders; Python's `glob` skips such names). Over all 26,503, the translator's own marker test (`WAVE64_RE`, or a 64-bit op on EXEC/VCC) on the code `translate()` keeps finds 21,554 wave64: 10,031 PS, 9,282 mesh shaders, 1,179 VS, 867 CS and 195 GS. With the 2,287 above that is exactly the flag's 23,841, and none contradicts it; read raw, 104 more wave32 listings (80 CS, 24 PS) seem to carry a marker, all from embedded data words decoded as instructions. The first marker scan (`s_mov_b64 exec, -1`, `exec_hi`, `s_and/or_saveexec_b64` and 64-bit masks, written into this row at `0082ad53`) counted 9,930 PS, 8,666 mesh shaders, 1,115 VS and 923 CS: 20,634 without the GS, so not the 21,54