r's I/J operands are not read: the weights come from `BaryCoordKHR` (213 PS that compute their own barycentrics into v0/v1 first are a separate gap). Confirmed by `tests/semantic/test_interp_f16_high.py` (per-vertex halves 0.5/1.5/2.5 and −4/8/16 at weights (0.25, 0.25, 0.5) give 1.75 and 9.0; fails on the old translation) and the GPU fixture `ps_interp_f16_high` drawn with `vs_f16_pack` (u and v packed in one channel both ways, f32 control) and, through `scripts/run_gpu_tests.py`, with the mesh producer `ms_f16_pack` (mesh `exp paramN` stores the same raw words, `Gen.ms_export`): both pass on an RTX 5070 and fail on the old translation (off by 0.984). spirv-val and `vk_pipeline_check` (real pipelines against the vec4 VS stubs, RTX 5070) also accept PS mixing high, low, f32 and flat reads of one location and of several. **Measured natively on RDNA2** (AMD Radeon 680M, `gfx1035`, wave32 and wave64 via `tools/vinterp_hw_probe.py --oracle both`, SHADER-430): the VINTRP unit reads `P0`, `P10` and `P20` from LDS at `M0 + 48·attr + {8·c, 8·c + 4, 32 + 4·c}`; `v_interp_p1ll_f16` (and `v_interp_p1lv_f16`, taking `P0.half` from `vsrc2`) widens the selected f16 half (`[15:0]` or `[31:16]` with `high`) to binary32 and writes the fused binary32 `fma(i, f32(P10.half), f32(P0.half))`, while `v_interp_p2_f16` rounds the exact `partial_f32 + j · f32(P20.half)` once to f16 following `MODE.FP_ROUND.16_64` (like `v_fma_mixlo_f16`, whereas the translator narrows the f32 sum) and writes `vdst[15:0]` while hardware keeps `vdst[31:16]`; f16 subnormal halves are preserved with `denorm_mode_16_64 = 3` (`0x0001 + 0.5·0x0200 + 0.5·0x0200 → 0x0201`, flushed to `±0` when `0`), and f16 NaN halves quiet with payload preserved (`0x7e05 → 0x7e05`, `0x7c01 → 0x7e01`). **ALU half measured on AMD hardware** (SHADER-430, `tools/interp_hw_probe.py`, gfx1036, every case matched; `docs/shader_validation.md`): `p1ll_f16` widens the chosen f16 halves exactly and computes fma(h10, I, h0) with one rounding to f32. `p2_f16` computes fma(h20, J, p1ll) and rounds it once, straight to f16: 30 hand-built ties gave that result and never f32-then-f16. It keeps vdst's high 16 bits. The translator used to zero them and now keeps them (`write_lo16`; `test_p2_f16_keeps_the_high_half_of_vdst`). **Still unverified:** what the SPI writes into LDS for a packed channel in a real draw (P10/P20 as f32 differences of the raw words, or per-half f16 differences under `FP16_INTERP_MODE`), which needs a pixel-shader harness; also f16 inf/NaN halves and `p1lv_f16`. **Unverified:** AMD hardware (whether it computes P10/P20 in f16 or rounds the p1ll intermediate differently), f16 denormal and NaN halves. **`--bary-fallback`** has no per-vertex inputs, so it keeps the old smooth-f32 read and cannot honour `high`; `check.py` skips the fixture there. MoltenVK can't compile `PerVertexKHR`: see the next row. |
-| 16-bit interpolation without `PerVertexKHR` (`--f16-interp-fallback`) | MoltenVK reports `fragmentShaderBarycentric` but can't create a pipeline with the row above's inputs: its SPIR-V to MSL conversion stops with "PerVertexKHR decoration is not supported in MSL" (MoltenVK 1.4.2 on an Apple A18 Pro and, reported by @DomasUrbonas in #166, an M4 Pro), so `ps_interp_f16_high` failed on every Mac. With the flag no `PerVertexKHR` (and, for these reads, no `BaryCoordKHR`) is used: every VS `exp paramN`, mesh param and `--mesh-compute` vertex (`tools/mesh_compute_vs.py`) also exports the channel's two f16 halves decoded to f32 (`UnpackHalf2x16`), low halves at location 28 − 2N and high halves at 29 − 2N, so nothing goes above location 29: MoltenVK's `maxVertexOutputComponents` is 124 because Metal counts `gl_Position` in its 128 vertex-output scalars, and the Khronos validation layer counts it too, so a VS output at location 30 failed VUID-RuntimeSpirv-Location-06272 on `vs_ids` (SHADER-291); a PS `*_f16` read of attrN.c takes component c of that ordinary smooth input, then p2 clamps and narrows as before. Interpolation is linear, so the device interpolating the decoded halves gives the same P0 + i(P1 − P0) + j(P2 − P0) as the per-vertex path, with the interpolator's rounding instead of explicit f32 arithmetic. f32 and `v_interp_mov_f32` reads of such a location are the raw word as on any location (flat = the provoking vertex's word, exact). A producer leaves out the halves of a param whose locations would land on one of its own params (params 0..P−1 leave room for the halves of params 0..(28 − P) / 2), and when a PS's canonical decoded-half locations collide with an attribute it reads, its halves are packed into free locations instead (dynamic packing, below). The index records `f16_halves` ({param: [low, high location]}) on both sides; a pipeline links when the PS's entries are among its producer's. Chosen like the wave64 mode: `tools/vk_subgroup_info.py` reports `per_vertex_inputs` (false without `fragmentShaderBarycentric` or on driver ID MoltenVK), and `make_game.py` and `run_gpu_tests.py` turn the flag on where it is false (`LEONIDA_F16_INTERP_FALLBACK=1`/`0` overrides the runner; `build.py` takes only the variable, since it may run in WSL). Without the flag the translation is unchanged: all 139 GPU-test modules are byte-identical to main's, with and without `--bary-fallback` and with `--mesh-compute --wave64-emulate`, so NVIDIA and AMD keep the exact per-vertex path. Confirmed on an Apple A18 Pro (macOS 26.6.2, MoltenVK 1.4.2): `ps_interp_f16_high` drawn with `vs_f16_pack` passes natively and with wave64 emulation, every one of its 4,096 pixels bit-exact to f16 of the exact u and v in both halves (it failed before at pipeline creation), and `ms_f16_pack` through `--mesh-compute` (`LEONIDA_MESH_COMPUTE=1`) passes too; `tests/semantic/test_f16_interp_fallback.py` checks the PS reads (1.75 and 9.0 from the decoded halves), the VS exports (one-lane interpreter), the overlap rules and the default being unchanged. **Unverified:** a PS whose producer exports more params than leave room for the halves it reads (it reads that producer's param, not halves: the `f16_halves` entries then don't match); devices with fewer than 31 output locations. CPU check (TEST-398, `FallbackStagePair`): with ordinary VS exports at 28 and 29, the PS reads those values (5 and 7 in the test) in place of attr0's decoded halves (2 and 3). Only the PS lists attr0 in `f16_halves`. With param28 as the last export, location 28 carries its ordinary value and location 29 has no VS output. When the last ordinary export is at 27, both stages list the pair and the PS reads 2 and 3. The move from 30 to 29 (SHADER-291) was checked on an Apple M5 (macOS 27.2.1, MoltenVK 1.4.2, Khronos validation layer 1.4.363): over the 170 GPU-test modules `vk_pipeline_check` reported VUID-RuntimeSpirv-Location-06272 on 6 vertex shaders at location 30 and on none at 29; the same 15 modules list `f16_halves` for the same params, `run_gpu_tests.py` gives the same result per test, and `ps_interp_f16_high` reads back byte-identical pixels. **Dynamic packing and per-pipeline PSO linking (SHADER-378):** when a PS's canonical `28 − 2N`/`29 − 2N` slots collide with its raw attributes (266 corpus PS where f16-read attrs reach `n >= 7` and raw attrs reach 12–14, all with `2·\|F\| <= 29 − max`) or fall below 0, `assign_f16_half_locations` in `tools/rdna2spirv.py` packs all `f16_halves` pairs downward from location 29 into locations disjoint from its raw attributes (keeping the canonical slots byte-identical whenever collision-free, and refusing only genuine `> 30` location budget overflows); `tools/mesh_compute_vs.py` adds `pair_f16_halves` and an optional `f16_halves` override to `vs_spvasm`; and at graphics PSO creation `runtime/src/shader_library.h` loads `f16_halves` while `runtime/src/spirv_f16_halves.h` (called from `runtime/shims/d3d12_vulkan_device.cpp`) fast-paths exact matches, relocates PS/VS `Location` decorations when needed, demotes unneeded VS static half outputs to `Private`, and synthesizes `UnpackHalf2x16` half exports before `OpReturn` for params a high-param producer (`param0..15`) could not emit statically. Checked by `tests/semantic/test_f16_interp_fallback.py`, `tests/semantic/test_interp_p1lv_f16.py`, `tests/test_mesh_compute_vs.py`, `runtime/test/shader_library_test.cpp`, `runtime/test/spirv_f16_halves_test.cpp`, and `runtime/test/d3d12_f16_halves_test.cpp` (real Vulkan 1.3 draw on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Ti, driver 32.0.16.1088, Windows 11: 16-param VS + colliding PS both synthesized and relocated render exact RGBA `(64, 191, 127, 255)`). **Unverified:** native MoltenVK validation run on macOS and full-corpus recount under `--f16-interp-fallback` (needs game copy); native `MeshEXT` runtime PSO specialization (mesh PSOs are not on `main` yet); devices with fewer than 124 vertex output components. Combined with `--bary-fallback`'s geometry shader (`tools/rdna2spirv.py` allocates `%bary` outside all occupied read and reserved f16-half slots; covered by `tests/semantic/test_bary_fallback.py`, and all `3/3` whole-corpus shaders combining both draw `11/11` targets bit-exact on an RX 6650 XT). |
