s_addc`, is tier C; a vote is on the mask register's value when it was taken, so a new value in that register before the branch (`v_cmp` into the pair or VCC, `s_mov_b64`, `s_cselect_b64`: none of them writes SCC) ends it, and the branch is judged as a plain SCC branch, SHADER-820), whole-quad masks, `ds_bpermute`/`v_permlane16` (they stay inside a half, not GPU-tested here: no fixture; `s_cbranch_scc*` after a vote is the same kind of skip, any other reader of that SCC, `s_cselect`/`s_cmov`/`s_addc`, is tier C), whole-quad masks, `ds_bpermute`/`v_permlane16` (they stay inside a half, not GPU-tested here: no fixture; `ds_swizzle` and `v_permlanex16` likewise; GPU-checked at required 64 and 32 lanes by `ps_w64_a_bpermute`, `ps_w64_a_permlane` (`v_permlane16` and `v_permlanex16`) and `ps_w64_a_swizzle` in `w64_graphics.py`, TEST-433), loops closed by `execnz` with no scalar state carried. `ds_swizzle` and `v_permlanex16` likewise), loops closed by `execnz` with no scalar state carried. A `vccz`/`vccnz` on a wave-uniform compare is no lane vote and is tier A too: a `v_cmp` made while EXEC is the launch mask from untainted immediates, scalars that are no lane mask and VGPRs written from them (optionally ANDed with EXEC, the LLVM form `s_and_b64 vcc, exec, s[..]`) gives every launch lane or none, so each half votes as the wave does and both sides may have code and scalars of their own (if/else, a uniform loop back edge; the mask itself read as data, e.g. exported, is still tier C `cross-lane`), while the same compare under a narrowed EXEC (a half with no active lane votes no and keeps its own scalars) or on a tainted value (`v_readfirstlane` of a varying VGPR, a lane count, an AND with a lane mask) stays tier C; confirmed on the wave interpreter (`UniformVote` in `tests/semantic/test_wave64_halves.py`: 64 lanes against two waves of 32, equal for the accepted forms with s4 = 9 and s4 = 1, different for each near miss) and by `ps_w64_a_uniform_vote`, which gives its derived words on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 (Linux, driver 580.65.06, 32 only). **B**: also a waterfall loop (`v_readfirstlane` of a varying value, `v_cmp_eq` against the same VGPR, `saveexec`, body, `s_xor exec`, `execnz`; the scalar only used inside the body), a wave-aggregated atomic (`s_bcnt1` of EXEC, `v_mbcnt` rank of that same EXEC, one `buffer_atomic_add` of that count (the count of the mask the rank ranks) by the rank-0 lane only, `v_readfirstlane` of the result with EXEC restored, base + rank as a store address, the rank counting the very mask the atomic's count was taken of (a rank of the entry EXEC or of a saved copy next to a base of the narrowed one is tier C `lane-rank`); the rank alone or the base alone is not a slot: exact as a set of slots, **the order of the slots within a wave is not preserved**, a 64-lane wave takes one run of slots, two halves take two), `v_readfirstlane` / `v_readlane` of a VGPR written from scalars and constants while EXEC was the launch mask (the same in every lane), a `v_writelane` / `v_readlane` spill pair on one constant lane below 32. **C**: anything else that reaches an export, a store's data, a branch or EXEC: lane count / rank / first-lane index / raw lane id (`v_mbcnt` of -1), `v_readlane` of a varying value at any lane (on a wave64 every lane gets hardware lane N, in halves each half gets its own lane N, so lane 7 is as wrong as lane 40; the research note's row 9 is too generous here); an analysis failure is tier C with reason `analysis-error` (the translation does not fail), `v_writelane` to a non-constant or >= 32 lane, a 32-bit access to half of a lane mask (`exec_hi`, `vcc_hi`, a mask pair's odd register), a mask read as data (including a mask combined by `s_and/or/andn2_b64` with a register that is not one: a user value, a loaded mask), a scalar defined behind a wave-wide vote and read where it may not have run (`s_add s5, s5, 1` after `s_cbranch_vccz`; a trip count in an SGPR of a divergent loop: the halves vote differently), a branch on a count, an elected-lane side effect that is not a count, `ds_append`/`ds_consume`. The check is conservative: unknown means C. `--wave64-graphics=warn` (default) marks tier C and counts it in the summary line, `error` refuses it (`Unsupported`), `off` skips the analysis; `python tools/wave64_halves.py --census <spirv>/index.json` prints tier, reason and idiom counts (no names); it complements `tools/wave64_graphics_scan.py` (#1132), which counts candidate cross-half opcodes in the disassembly before translation and decides nothing. **Corpus census** (1.0.2.1, all 26,503 shaders translated at `main` `f847748c` with `--wave64-emulate`, `--census` over that index): 13,405 wave64 graphics shaders (2,236 VS, 10,974 PS, 195 GS, the FSSM header's wave size, see *Wave size*); tier A 4,033 (30.1%), B 85 (0.6%), C 9,287 (69.3%): VS 1,455/79/702, PS 2,383/6/8,585, GS 195/0/0. Tier C reasons (a shader can have several): unsafe-vote-branch 8,285, tainted-branch 6,547, tainted-exec 5,189, cross-lane 4,764, stale-scalar 3,415, mask-half 759, unsafe-execz-branch 583, first-lane 353, waterfall-scalar 161, lane-id 152, lane-count 1. Idiom tags, counted on every shader that carries one whatever its tier (`--census` prints them as "tier B idioms"; 85 shaders are tier B): readfirstlane-uniform 626, lane-spill 287, waterfall 35. (Earlier censuses counted 11,405 shaders, from when the wave size came from code markers: TEST-319 had A 1,919, B 17, C 9,469 with all 195 GS in C; its re-run with the GS ring rule below and #1982 had A 2,117, B 17, C 9,271 with all 195 GS in A.) The lane-id bpermute rule moves no corpus shader. (The TEST-319 census, from when the wave size came from code markers and before the GS ring-address rule below, counted 11,405 shaders: A 1,919, B 17, C 9,469, with all 195 GS in C.) The lane-id bpermute rule moves no corpus shader. **Corpus census** (TEST-319, 1.0.2.1, `--census` over the translated index, re-run with the GS ring rule below and #1982): 11,405 wave64 graphics shaders (1,179 VS, 10,031 PS, 195 GS); tier A 2,117 (18.6%), B 17 (0.1%), C 9,271 (81.3%): VS 479/14/686, PS 1,443/3/8,585, GS 195/0/0. Tier C reasons (a shader can have several): unsafe-vote-branch 8,285, tainted-branch 6,550, tainted-exec 5,189, cross-lane 4,760, stale-scalar 3,415, mask-half 758, unsafe-execz-branch 583, first-lane 353, waterfall-scalar 161, lane-id 152, lane-count 1. 11,405 wave64 graphics shaders (1,179 VS, 10,031 PS, 195 GS); tier A 1,919 (16.8%), B 17 (0.1%), C 9,469 (83.0%): VS 465/14/700, PS 1,454/3/8,574, GS 0/0/195. Tier C reasons (a shader can have several): unsafe-vote-branch 8,175, tainted-branch 6,582, cross-lane 6,111, tainted-exec 4,959, stale-scalar 3,162, mask-half 758, unsafe-execz-branch 583, first-lane 353, lane-id 277, waterfall-scalar 167, lane-count 1. Tier B idioms: readfirstlane-uniform 558, lane-spill 287, waterfall 35. The lane-id bpermute rule moves no corpus shader. **Corpus coverage is not measured here**: it needs a run over the game's shaders by someone who has them. **Rewrite (the only one the emission needs)**: `v_readlane_b32` of a proven-uniform VGPR reads the lane's own copy instead of shuffling from lane N, because a subgroup shuffle from lane >= 32 of a 32-wide subgroup is undefined (143 of 144 test shaders translate byte-identically to origin/main, only `ps_w64_b_readlane_uniform` changed). Everything else tier B needs is what the translator already emits per subgroup: the ballot/count/rank/first-lane words of `wave_mask` are the subgroup's own (the high word is 0), `v_readfirstlane` is the subgroup's first active lane, a waterfall runs once per distinct value of the subgroup. GPU-checked on AMD Radeon 880M (driver 26.11.2; `requiredSubgroupSizeStages` has FRAGMENT, size 32..64) with `runtime/test/shaders/w64_graphics.py`: each `ps_w64_*` fixture drawn with the fragment subgroup size required to 64 (one hardware wave) and to 32 (two halves). `ps_w64_a_skip`, `_b_waterfall`, `_b_readfirstlane`, `_b_readlane_uniform` and `_b_ballot_atomic` (counter 1024 and the 1024 slot words as a set; slot order differs) match each other and the words their headers derive; `_c_bcnt_export`, `_c_lane_id`, `_c_readlane`, `_c_vote_stale`, `_c_vote_else`, `_c_scc_vote`, `_c_execz_sgpr` (an SGPR written on the exec-zero side and exported) and `_c_waterfall_count` differ between 64 and 32 (the detector is right). `ps_w64_a_one_lane` is the case where only one pixel votes yes (so one half votes and the other does not, in the wave and in the halves): its skip regions (`execz`, `vccz`, `vccnz` with a jump-only fall-through) give equal images at 64 and 32; `_c_vote_else` (code on the vote's no side) and `_c_scc_vote` (SCC of a vote as data) differ. `ps_w64_a_discard` (three kills, two of them dropping whole regions so that some halves lose every pixel while the other half of their wave lives) was drawn on an AMD Radeon RX 580 (RADV, Mesa 26.0.8, required size 64, saved with `W64G_SAVE_REF`) and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 (32 only, driver 620.73.04, compared with `W64G_REF`): both equal the derived words, discarded pixels keeping the clear value, and each other. NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop (32 only, driver 617.42): tier A/B fixtures equal the derived words and the 880M@64 images (`W64G_SAVE_REF` / `W64G_REF`), tier C fixtures differ from the 880M@64 images. `ps_w64_a_permlane`, `_a_swizzle` and `_a_bpermute` (TEST-319: permlane16 neighbour selects, permlanex16 there and back, swizzle quad-permute, swap and broadcast modes, bpermute from lane-id addresses; every read stays in the pixel's 2x2 quad or comes back, so the words do not depend on how pixels are packed into lanes) give the derived words on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 (Linux, driver 620.73.04, 32 only). With an AMD Radeon RX 580 (Polaris, RADV Mesa 26.0.8) as the 64-lane reference (`W64G_SAVE_REF`; GCN has one subgroup size, 64, in every stage but no FRAGMENT in `requiredSubgroupSizeStages`, so `vk_harness` counts a requested size equal to a device's only size as applied), the RTX 5070 run of every fixture (`W64G_REF`) equals the reference for all 13 tier A/B fixtures and differs from it for all 11 tier C ones, pixel and vertex; the same reads in a vertex shader run on the wave interpreter (64 lanes against two waves of 32, equal) because a GPU's partial vertex waves leave the lanes they would read empty. Vertex shaders: the VS stage cannot be forced on the 880M (no VERTEX in `requiredSubgroupSizeStages`), so VS is covered by the strict wave interpreter (`tests/semantic/test_wave64_halves.py`: 64 lanes against two waves of 32; tier A/B equal, tier C differs); geometry shaders are not tested (the corpus ones are wave64, see *Wave size*; no fixture). 64 lanes against two waves of 32; tier A/B equal, tier C differs), and on RADV also on the GPU (TEST-251): RADV runs the VS stage (NGG) as wave64 by default and as wave32 with `RADV_PERFTEST=gewave32` (GFX10+, seen in the `RADV_DEBUG=shaders` dump: 64-bit EXEC operations against `exec_lo`), so `w64_graphics.py` draws each `vs_w64_*` fixture both ways, 960 zero-area vertices (`VKH_VERTICES`) that store four words each to the heap, with `vs_w64_c_lane_id` as the probe that the wave size changed (largest lane id 63, then 31). RADV packs 62 or 64 vertices into a wave64 and 30 or 32 into a wave32, so the 32-lane run repacks the vertices rather than halving the waves; tier A/B words are keyed on the vertex index, which makes this the stricter test. `vs_w64_a_skip`, `_b_waterfall`, `_b_readlane_uniform` (readfirstlane and readlane 40/63 of uniform VGPRs) and `_b_ballot_atomic` (240 slots as a set) give the derived words at both sizes; `_c_bcnt_export`, `_c_readlane` and `_c_lane_id` differ (the detector is right), on an RX 9070 XT (GFX1201) and a Ryzen 9800X3D iGPU (GFX1036, RDNA2), RADV Mesa 26.3.1; geometry shaders are not tested (no fixture; the corpus has 195 wave64 geometry shaders, all tier C, see the census). `_c_bcnt_export`, `_c_readlane` and `_c_lane_id` differ (the detector is right), on an RX 9070 XT (GFX1201) and a Ryzen 9800X3D iGPU (GFX1036, RDNA2), RADV Mesa 26.3.1; geometry shaders likewise (TEST-251): RADV runs the GS stage (NGG) as wave64 by default and as wave32 with `gewave32`, and `w64_graphics.py` draws each `gs_w64_*` fixture after `vs_ids` (960 vertices, 320 triangles, `VKH_GS`), each invocation finding its triangle from `vs_ids`'s vertex number and storing four words to the heap, with `gs_w64_c_lane_id` as the probe (largest lane id 41, RADV puts at most 42 GS invocations in a wave64, then 31). **LDS writes of a legacy GS:** `rdna2spirv.gs_ds` maps one to an output slot from its offset alone and drops the VGPR address (the lane's GSVS area), so `wave64_halves.py` checks only the data a GS `ds_write` writes, not its address; before, a lane id that only formed the GSVS base made the shader tier C `lane-id`. Confirmed three ways: a hand-written GS with the base `lane id * 96` translates, after `spirv-opt -O`, to the same instructions as without it; `gs_w64_a_ring` (that shape) gives the derived records at wave64 and wave32 on both GPUs; and `tests/semantic/test_wave64_halves.py` (`GeometryRing`) keeps the lane id in the written data, or in a memory store's address, tier C. The corpus GS were that false tier C: all 195 contain `v_mbcnt_hi`, all 195 were tier C in the TEST-319 census, and `wave64_halves.py --census` on the corpus translated at `f847748c` puts all 195 in tier A. `_c_bcnt_export`, `_c_readlane` and `_c_lane_id` differ (the detector is right), on an RX 9070 XT (GFX1201) and a Ryzen 9800X3D iGPU (GFX1036, RDNA2), RADV Mesa 26.3.1; geometry shaders are not tested (the corpus ones are wave64, see *Wave size*; no fixture). The corpus GS form their GSVS base from `v_mbcnt_lo/hi` (all 195 contain `v_mbcnt_hi`): they were all a false tier C, and the census above has all 195 at tier A. `wave64_halves.py --census` on a translated corpus shows it. `_c_bcnt_export`, `_c_readlane` and `_c_lane_id` differ (the detector is right), on an RX 9070 XT (GFX1201) and a Ryzen 9800X3D iGPU (GFX1036, RDNA2), RADV Mesa 26.3.1; geometry shaders are not tested (the corpus ones are wave32; no fixture). Not checked: helper lanes of partial quads; `VK_KHR_shader_maximal_reconvergence` is assumed for waterfall and election loops. Tiers A and B assume subgroups of at least 32 lanes: a fragment stage may run fewer live lanes than the subgroup size it reports (Intel UHD 770: gl_SubgroupSize 32, ballot count 16, a shuffle from lane L^16 returns garbage; measured by kapitche, TEST-433), so `w64_graphics.py` skips the tier A/B pixel fixtures on a device whose minimum subgroup size is below 32 when neither the 64 nor the 32 run took effect; deriving the skip from a measured live fragment width (ballot count) in `vk_harness` is a follow-up. **ADD_TID_ENABLE** (SHADER-833, finding by @shdann in #4571): an access with no `idxen` through a V# whose word3 is a literal (`s_mov_b32` earlier in the block, the notion `rdna2spirv` uses for `sgpr_imm`) with bit 23 set takes the wave lane as its index (`idxen` ignores the bit, see *Swizzled buffer addressing*), so its `buffer_load*`/`tbuffer_load*` result and its `buffer_store*`/`tbuffer_store*`/`buffer_atomic*` address carry the raw lane id: tier C `lane-id`. A literal with the bit clear, and every access with `idxen`, are unchanged. **Assumption**: through a run-time V# (loaded from memory or user data) tiers A and B take ADD_TID_ENABLE as 0 for an access without `idxen`; the code cannot say otherwise. The literal is tracked with the same folds `rdna2spirv` applies to `sgpr_imm` (mirrored in `Analysis.literal_sgpr`: `s_mov_b32/b64`, `s_movk_i32`, `s_pack_*`, `s_bfm_b32`, `s_not_b32`, `s_and/lshl/lshr_b32`, `s_mul_i32`; other folded ops count as run-time). The corpus census was not re-measured with this rule: its tier counts above are from before it and may change. Follow-up, needs the game: count the bound V#s with word3 bit 23 set at run time, and re-run `--census`. |
 | GFX10.1 (console gfx1013) / GFX10.3 (gfx1030, gfx1036 oracle) opcode differences | Found by disassembling every opcode of every GFX10 encoding (SOP1/SOP2/SOPK/SOPC/SOPP, SMEM, VOP1/VOP2/VOPC/VOP3/VOP3P, DS, FLAT/GLOBAL/SCRATCH, MUBUF/MTBUF, MIMG) with LLVM 21 `llvm-mc` for `-mcpu=gfx1013` and for `gfx1030`/`gfx1036` (the two gfx103x lists are identical). Every other opcode slot decodes to the same mnemonic on both, so the hardware-oracle rows measured on gfx1036 are about the same instructions the console runs (the microarchitectures still differ, which no opcode table shows). **On gfx1013 only:** `v_mac_f32`/`v_mad_f32` (VOP2 0x1f, VOP3 0x11f/0x141) and `v_mac_legacy_f32`/`v_mad_legacy_f32` (VOP2 0x06, VOP3 0x106/0x140), translated (rows above; not measurable on gfx103x, where 0x06/0x106/0x140 are the fused `v_fmac_legacy_f32`/`v_fma_legacy_f32`); `v_mul_lo_i32` (VOP3 0x16b; `Unsupported` until #3504 translates it); `s_get_waveid_in_workgroup` (SMEM 0x2a), translated in compute and mesh shaders; scalar stores (`s_store_dword*`, `s_buffer_store_dword*`), scalar atomics (`s_atomic_*`, `s_buffer_atomic_*`, 32- and 64-bit), scalar scratch (`s_scratch_load/store_dword*`), `s_dcache_wb`, `s_dcache_discard(_x2)` and the 31 LDS `ds_*_src2_*` forms, all `Unsupported`. **On gfx103x only:** `v_dot2c_f32_f16`, `v_dot4c_i32_i8`, the VOP3P `v_dot2_f32_f16`, `v_dot2_i32_i16`/`u32_u16`, `v_dot4_i32_i8`/`u32_u8`, `v_dot8_i32_i4`/`u32_u4`, `v_fmac_legacy_f32`/`v_fma_legacy_f32`, `global_load/store_dword_addtid`, `global_atomic_csub`, `buffer_atomic_csub`. The console's code can't contain these; the translator handles the integer dot products (rows above, measured on gfx1036) and refuses the rest, so a listing disassembled for the wrong target (VOP2 0x06 printed as `v_fmac_legacy_f32`) fails loudly instead of being translated as a fused multiply-add. Not covered by the sweep: opcodes whose operand template did not decode on either target, and behaviour that differs without an opco